Post-AGI Alpha Hunt =E2=80=94 10 Sep 2026

Highest-signal new rabbit holes from this run

   1. InP crystal-growth equipment may be a better bottleneck than InP
   epitaxy itself

The AI-optics thesis has moved one layer upstream. Recent industry
reporting estimates 2026 effective InP substrate capacity at only
~0.6=E2=80=930.75M wafers versus ~2.6=E2=80=933.0M wafers of demand, with 2=
-inch
optical-grade substrate prices rising from about $800 in 2025 to roughly
$2,300=E2=80=932,500 by April 2026. Large-diameter crystal growth is diffic=
ult,
yields take years to improve, and customer qualification adds latency.

PVA TePla (ETR:TPE) is particularly interesting because it sells the VGF
crystal-growth equipment used for 4=E2=80=936 inch GaAs/InP crystals. Its Q=
1 2026
order intake rose 164% YoY to =E2=82=AC121.6M, and management explicitly id=
entified
initial equipment orders for InP crystal production tied to AI data
centers. H1 reporting later showed =E2=82=AC186.7M order intake (+80%), inc=
luding
InP crystal-growth systems and HBM ultrasonic metrology.

Why this may be alpha: the market sees =E2=80=9Coptics demand=E2=80=9D; the=
 scarcer
economic node may be the equipment that expands qualified bulk-substrate
capacity. This is picks-and-shovels beneath the picks-and-shovels.

Falsifiers: InP is displaced rapidly by silicon photonics/external laser
architectures that materially reduce InP consumption; 6-inch yields ramp
much faster than expected; Chinese equipment vendors commoditize VGF
systems; PVA=E2=80=99s orders fail to convert into revenue/margin.

Sources:
https://www.pvatepla.com/corporate/investorrelations/financial-news/pva-tep=
la-off-to-a-strong-start-to-the-year/
https://www.pvatepla.com/products-technologies/crystal-growth/vertical-grad=
ient-freeze/kronos/
https://en.etnews.com/20260708200006
https://www.trendforce.com/news/2026/08/06/news-inp-shortage-emerges-as-ai-=
optical-interconnect-bottleneck/

   1. Non-Chinese indium recovery is becoming strategic optionality

China produces roughly 70% of global indium and has tightened scrutiny
around indium/InP exports. Reuters reported in June that InP had already
been placed under Chinese export controls and that the U.S. Defense
Logistics Agency is preparing to stockpile up to 403 tons of indium over
three years.

Korea Zinc (KRX:010130) may be a hidden exposure. It is Korea=E2=80=99s onl=
y
domestic indium producer and reportedly produces around 90=E2=80=93100 tons
annually. In Q2 2026 it increased indium sales volume 51% QoQ while prices
had roughly doubled from the prior year; indium=E2=80=99s share of its rare=
-metals
revenue more than doubled. The company is also considering moving
downstream into indium compounds for data-center customers.

The deeper thesis is supply elasticity: indium is largely a by-product of
zinc refining, so price cannot summon new primary mine output quickly. A
recycler/smelter with proprietary recovery technology can therefore own
convex optionality without needing to predict which laser vendor wins.

Falsifiers: Chinese export friction eases; optical architectures materially
reduce indium intensity; zinc-smelting byproduct recovery scales quickly
elsewhere; indium prices collapse as inventories normalize.

Sources:
https://www.reuters.com/world/china/china-tightens-indium-export-checks-ai-=
demand-increases-2026-06-19/
https://en.sedaily.com/finance/2026/08/17/korea-zinc-cashes-in-on-indium-pr=
ice-surge-driven-by-ai
https://www.reuters.com/world/asia-pacific/korea-zinc-talks-with-us-tech-fi=
rms-extract-rare-earths-data-centre-waste-2026-03-12/

   1. =E2=80=9CMeasurement density=E2=80=9D may rise faster than wafer star=
ts

INFICON (SIX:IFCN) is a useful test case for a broader factor: as
semiconductor process windows narrow and tools become more complex, the
number/value of measurements required per manufacturing step rises. This
can create growth even without proportional wafer-volume growth.

INFICON=E2=80=99s Q2 2026 sales reached a record $198.1M (+18.3% YoY); H1
Semiconductor & Vacuum Coating revenue rose 27.2%. Order intake hit an
all-time high, and management raised FY2026 guidance to $750=E2=80=93780M r=
evenue
with a 19=E2=80=9320% operating margin. The company cites leading-edge logi=
c, HBM,
data-center infrastructure and increased sensor usage at advanced nodes as
drivers.

The concept to add to the kernel is Measurement Density =3D measurements /
process step =C3=97 process steps / device =C3=97 devices produced. If AI h=
ardware
complexity increases the first two terms, metrology/process-control
suppliers can outgrow the underlying wafer market.

Falsifiers: integrated tool vendors internalize sensing; process
simplification offsets node complexity; Chinese sensor substitutes compress
pricing; semiconductor capex turns before measurement intensity rises.

Sources:
https://www.inficon.com/en/news/inficon-delivers-record-second-quarter-2026=
-and-raises-full-year-guidance

   1. High-vacuum infrastructure is a cross-world necessity, but
   crowdedness matters

VAT Group=E2=80=99s Q1 2026 semiconductor valve orders rose 64.5% YoY and b=
acklog
rose 24.9%. VAT explicitly says AI-driven fab expansion, leading-edge logic
and HBM are underpinning a structural equipment cycle, with more than 110
fabs under construction globally.

This is less obscure than PVA/INFICON, so the alpha may be in second-tier
or component suppliers rather than VAT itself. But the kernel should treat
ultra-high-vacuum integrity as a cross-world necessity across advanced
semiconductors, quantum systems, thin-film optics and scientific
instrumentation.

Useful feature: Vacuum Criticality =3D vacuum steps =C3=97 contamination
sensitivity =C3=97 downtime cost =C3=97 leak-detection intensity.

Falsifiers: tool architecture consolidates vacuum subsystems; fab-capex
cycle rolls over; local Chinese vacuum suppliers take high-end share faster
than expected.

Sources:
https://www.vatgroup.com/news/medienmitteilung-zum-lagebericht-q1-2026
https://www.vatgroup.com/investor-relations/annual-report/outlook

   1. Humanoid robotics may already be migrating from reducer scarcity to
   precision-machining/measurement scarcity

The obvious trade is harmonic/RV reducers. The less obvious derivative is
what constrains reducer capacity expansion: ultra-precision grinding,
encoder gratings, bearings, metrology and force-torque sensor
manufacturing. Industry reporting shows reducer orders booked into 2027
while Chinese localization is already increasing quickly, implying that
simply owning reducer makers risks being late to the bottleneck.

The research direction should therefore be: reducer demand -> capacity
additions -> precision-machine-tool and inspection demand. This is exactly
the bottleneck-derivative framework: find what becomes scarce when today=E2=
=80=99s
shortage is successfully addressed.

Falsifiers: humanoid unit volumes remain mostly pilot-scale; direct-drive
actuators reduce reducer intensity; China overbuilds component capacity and
destroys margins before machine-tool suppliers monetize the cycle.

Source:
https://www.geyontech.com/tech-library/62/

Kernel additions from this run

   - bulk_crystal_growth_capacity
   - qualification_latency_by_wafer_diameter
   - byproduct_supply_elasticity
   - non_china_supply_share
   - measurement_density
   - vacuum_criticality
   - bottleneck_derivative_score
   - capacity_expansion_enabler

Highest-priority names for deeper valuation work: PVA TePla first; INFICON
second; Korea Zinc as a strategic-material optionality case; then search
globally for smaller suppliers to PVA/InP crystal-growth lines and
INFICON/VAT-adjacent vacuum/process-control components.

This memo is research, not investment advice.

